'''Python's heapq module is the only built-in way to implement a priority queue
queue.PriorityQueue is just a wrapper around heapq with thread-safety added
There's no separate "PriorityQueue" data structure that uses a different implementation


heapq is a binary min heap, period.

It uses a plain Python list as the underlying array
Every parent ≤ its children (min heap property)
Each node has at most 2 children (binary)
It's the same logic as your scratch implementation


One key thing — heapq only provides a min heap. If you want a max heap, you have to hack it:


import heapq

# Max heap workaround: negate the values
pq = []
heapq.heappush(pq, -5)
heapq.heappush(pq, -3)
heapq.heappush(pq, -8)

max_val = -heapq.heappop(pq)  # returns 8
Mapping your code to heapq:



VV.IMP Heap is not a sorted array

| Your method      | heapq equivalent                |
|------------------|---------------------------------|
| insert(key)      | heapq.heappush(heap, key)       |
| extractMin()     | heapq.heappop(heap)             |
| getMin()         | heap[0]                         |
| _heapifyUp       | Done internally by heappush     |
| _heapifyDown     | Done internally by heappop      |
| changeKey()      | No direct equivalent            |
| buildHeap()      | heapq.heapify(list)             |

So yes — heapq is just a well-optimized version of exactly what you built from scratch.'''